    },
    "three_estimators_smallest_k_side_by_side": {
      "definition": "Three different definitions of 'smallest k to rank the two models reliably', reported side by side per pair so they are not confused for the same quantity: (1) smallest_k_leaderboard_flip_below_0.05 -- smallest k where P(underdog beats favorite, favorite/underdog labeled from the same pooled sample being resampled) < 0.05. Biased low: the label and the resample share their data. (2) smallest_k_disagreement_probability_below_0.05 -- smallest k where P(two independent freshly-drawn k-occasion evaluations of the same two models name different winners) < 0.05. Not biased by shared-sample labeling; this is the number to trust for 'if I reran the eval, would I get the same answer'. (3) smallest_k_g_model_reaches_0.8_at_actual_n_tasks -- smallest k where the generalizability coefficient G_model(k, n_tasks) (pooled ANOVA variance components, reliability of the *whole model-level score*, not this specific pairwise comparison) reaches the conventional 0.8 threshold, at the actual number of tasks used (n_tasks=24). This one is per-corpus, not per-pair, so the same value is repeated for every pair below. If any of the three is not reachable within the k table computed (k=1..8 here), that is reported explicitly with a reason instead of a number -- see the reason fields in leaderboard_flip_smallest_k, disagreement_smallest_k, and g_model_smallest_k_at_actual_n_tasks above.",
